== The Use: Ten Helps Against All His Devices

#strong[The use of the point.]

IF Satan has such a world of devices and stratagems to ensnare and ruin the souls of men, then instead of wondering that so few are saved, sit down and wonder that any are saved, that any escape the snares of this cunning fowler, who spreads his nets and casts out his baits in all places, and in all cases and companies, etc.

But this is not the main thing that I intend to speak to. My main business will be to set before you some special rules and helps against all his devices, and the first is this.

If you would not be taken by any of Satan’s devices, then walk by rule. He who walks by rule walks most safely;#footnote[Prov 12:28. Gal 6:16. Prov 15:24.] he who walks by rule walks most honorably; he who walks by rule walks most sweetly. When men throw off the word, then God throws them off, and then Satan takes them by the hand and leads them into snares at his pleasure. He who thinks himself too good to be ruled by the word will be found too bad to be acknowledged by God; and if God does not and will not acknowledge him, Satan will overthrow him by his stratagems. Those who keep to the rule will be kept in the hour of temptation: “Because #footnote[Rev 3:10.] you have kept My word of patient endurance, I will also keep you from the hour of temptation that is about to come upon the whole world, to test those who dwell on the earth.”

2\. As you would not be taken with any of Satan’s #emph[devices,] beware of vexing#footnote[#emph[Spiritus Sanctus est res delicata,] the divine Spirit is a very tender thing; if you grieve him, he will certainly grieve and vex your precious souls. Lam 1:16.] and grieving the Holy Spirit of God. It is the Spirit of the Lord Jesus Christ who is best able to reveal Satan’s snares to us. It is he alone who can point out all Satan’s plots, and reveal all his methods, and enable men to escape the pits that Satan has dug for their precious souls. Ah souls! If you#super[+] set that sweet and blessed Spirit mourning, who alone can secure you from Satan’s depths, by whom will you be secured? Man is a weak creature, and in no way able to detect Satan’s snares or to avoid them, unless the Spirit of the Lord gives him skill and power. Therefore, whoever else is grieved, make sure the Spirit is not grieved by your gross sins, nor by your refusing#footnote[Isa 63:10. Ps 73:2, 3.] the heart-reviving medicines and comforts that he sets before you, nor by slighting and despising his gracious workings in others,#footnote[1 Thess 5:19. Acts 2:13.] nor by calling sincerity hypocrisy, and faith fancy, etc., nor by fathering on the Spirit the things that are the offspring and fruits of your own hearts. The Spirit of the Lord is your counselor, your comforter, your upholder, your strengthener. It is only the Spirit who makes a man too great for Satan to conquer. “Greater is He who is in you#super[+]#footnote[1 John 4:4.] than he who is in the world.”

Thirdly, if you would not be taken with any of Satan’s devices, then labor for more heavenly wisdom. Ah souls!#footnote[If men could only see the fair face of wisdom with mortal eyes, they would be in love with her, says Plato.] You#super[+] are much in the dark. You have only a little compared with what others have, and with what you might have had, had you not failed yourselves. There are many knowing souls, but only a few wise souls. There is often a great deal of knowledge where there is only a little wisdom to make use of that knowledge. Knowledge without wisdom#footnote[#emph[Sine prudentia simplicitas stultitia est] \[Without prudence, simplicity is folly\], Drusius.] is like mettle in a blind horse, which is often the cause of the rider’s fall and of his bones being knocked against the walls. It is not the most knowing Christian, but the wisest Christian, who sees, avoids and escapes Satan’s snares. “The path of life leads upward #footnote[Prov 15:24.] for the wise,” says Solomon, “that he may avoid going down to Sheol.” Heavenly wisdom makes a man delight to fly high; and the higher any man flies, the more he is out of the reach of Satan’s snares. Ah souls! You#super[+] need a great deal#footnote[#emph[Malim prudentiae guttam quam foecundioris fortunae pelagus] \[I would rather have a drop of prudence than an ocean of richer fortune\], said Nazianzen. A serpent’s eye is a rare ornament in a dove’s head.] of heavenly wisdom to see where and how Satan lays his baits and snares, and wisdom to find out proper #emph[remedies] against his #emph[devices,] and wisdom to apply those #emph[remedies] seasonably, inwardly and effectually to your own hearts, so that you may avoid the snares which that evil one has laid for your precious souls.

4\. If you would not be taken with any of Satan’s devices, then resist Satan’s first stirrings at once. It is safe to resist; it is dangerous to dispute. Eve disputes, and falls in Paradise;#footnote[Gen 3.] Job resists, and conquers on the dunghill. He who will play with Satan’s bait will quickly be taken with Satan’s hook. The promise of conquest is made to resisting, not to disputing: “Resist the devil, and he will flee #footnote[Jas 4:7.] from you#super[+].” Ah souls! If you#super[+] were better at resisting than at disputing (though perhaps you are not very expert at either), your temptations would be fewer, and your strength to stand would be greater than it now is, etc.

Fifthly, if you would not be taken with any of Satan’s #emph[devices,] then labor to be filled with the Spirit. The Spirit of the Lord is a Spirit of light and power, and what can a soul do without light, and power against “the spiritual forces of evil #footnote[Eph 6:12.] in the heavenly realms”? It is not enough that you have the Spirit; you must be filled with the Spirit, or else that evil spirit, Satan, will be too hard for you, and his plots will prosper against you. That is a sweet word of the apostle,#footnote[Eph 5:18 πληροῦσθε. To be filled with the Spirit, as the sails of a ship are filled with wind. Luther says that a holy gluttony is to fall to, to feed heartily, and to take deep drinks, until they are even drunk with loves and with the abundance of the Spirit; and oh, that there were more of such holy gluttony in the world!] “be filled with the Spirit”; that is, labor for an abundance of the Spirit. He who thinks he has enough of the Holy Spirit will quickly find himself overcome by the evil spirit. Satan has his snares to take you in prosperity and adversity, in health and sickness, in strength and weakness; when you are alone and when you are in company; when you come to spiritual duties and when you come away from spiritual duties. And if you are not filled with the Spirit, Satan will be too hard and too crafty for you, and will easily and frequently take you in his snares and make a prey of you in spite of yourselves. Therefore labor more to have your hearts filled with the Spirit than to have your heads filled with notions, your shops with goods, your chests with silver, or your bags with gold. So you will escape the snares of this fowler and triumph over all his plots, etc.

Sixthly, if you would not be taken in any of Satan’s snares, then keep humble.#footnote[It is reported of Satan that he said this of a learned man (#emph[tu me semper vincis]): “You always overcome me. When I would exalt and promote you, you keep yourself in humility; and when I would throw you down, you lift yourself up in assurance of faith.”] A humble heart will rather lie in the dust than rise by wickedness, and will sooner part with everything than with the peace of a good conscience. Humility keeps the soul free from many of the darts Satan casts and the snares he spreads, as the low shrubs are free from many violent gusts and blasts of wind that shake and tear the taller trees. The devil has least power to fasten a temptation on the man who is most humble. He who has a gracious measure of humility is neither moved by Satan’s offers nor terrified by his threats. I have read of one who, seeing in a vision many snares of the devil spread on the earth, sat down and mourned, and said to himself (#emph[Quis pertransiet ista]), “Who will pass through these?” To this he heard a voice answer (#emph[humilitas pertransiet]), “Humility will.” God has said that he will teach the humble,#footnote[Ps 25:9. Isa 57:15. Jas 4:6.] and that he will dwell with the humble, and that he will fill and satisfy the humble. And if the teachings of God, the indwellings of God, the pourings-in of God will not keep the soul from falling into Satan’s snares, I do not know what will. And therefore, as you would be happy in resisting Satan, and blessed in triumphing over Satan and all his snares, keep humble; I say again, keep humble, etc.

7\. If you would not be taken in any of Satan’s snares, then keep a strong, close and constant watch. A careless#footnote[1 Thess 5:6. We must not be like Agrippa’s dormouse, which would not wake until it was cast into boiling lead, but effectually heed the following Scriptures, in which this duty of watchfulness is so strictly commanded.] soul is already an ensnared soul. The soul that will not watch against temptations will certainly fall before the power of temptations. Satan works most strongly on the imagination when the soul is drowsy. The soul’s carelessness is Satan’s opportunity to fall on the soul and to plunder the soul, as Joshua did the men of Ai. The best way to be safe and secure from all Satan’s assaults is, with Nehemiah and the Jews, to watch and pray, and pray and watch. By this means they became too hard for their enemies, and the work of the Lord prospered sweetly in their hands. Remember how Christ rebuked his sluggish disciples: “What, were you#super[+] not able to keep watch with #footnote[Matt 24:42 ch. 26:41. Mark 13:33, 34, 35, 37. Luke 21:36.] Me for one hour?” “What, can you#super[+] not watch with me? How then will you#super[+] die with me?” If you cannot endure words, how will you endure wounds, etc.? Satan always keeps a crafty and malicious watch, “seeking someone to devour” (#emph[katapie]), or#footnote[1 Cor 16:13.] whom he may drink or sip up, as the#footnote[Col 4:2.] apostle speaks in 1 Pet 5:8. Satan#footnote[1 Pet 4:7. Rev 3:3.] is very envious of our condition, that we should enjoy that Paradise out of which he is cast, and out of which he will be kept forever.

Shall Satan keep a crafty watch, and#footnote[Hannibal never rested, whether he conquered or was conquered. It is so with Satan. “Learn, for shame, from the devil,” said blessed Latimer, “to watch, seeing the devil is so watchful.”] shall Christians not keep a holy, spiritual watch? Our whole life is beset with temptations. Satan watches every opportunity to break our peace, to wound our consciences, to lessen our comforts, to weaken our graces, to blur our evidences and to dampen our assurances, etc. Oh, what need then we have to be always on our watchtower, lest we be surprised by this subtle serpent! Watchfulness includes a waking, a rousing up of the soul. It is a continual, careful observing of our hearts and ways, in all the turnings of our lives, so that we always keep close to God and his word.

Watchfulness is nothing else but the soul running up and down, to and fro, busy everywhere. It is the heart busied and employed in diligent observation of (#emph[Quid inde]) what comes from within us, and of (#emph[Quid unde]) what comes from outside us and into us. Ah souls! You#super[+] are safe and secure only as long as you are on your watch. While Antipater kept the watch, Alexander was safe; and while we keep a strict watch, we are safe. A watchful soul is a soul on the wing, a soul out of gunshot, a soul on a rock, a soul in a castle, a soul above the clouds, a soul held fast in everlasting arms.

I will conclude this seventh head with this advice. Remember, the dragon is subtle: it bites the elephant’s ear and then sucks his blood, because it knows that to be the only place which the elephant cannot reach with his trunk to defend. So our enemies are so subtle that they will bite us and strike us where they can do us the most harm; and therefore it greatly concerns us to stand always on our guard.

Eighthly, if you would not be taken with any of Satan’s snares and devices, then keep up your communion with God. Your strength to stand against and withstand#footnote[2 Cor 6:16. The words are very significant in the original: there are two ins, as if God could never have near enough communion with them.] Satan’s fiery darts comes from your communion with God. A soul high in communion with God may be tempted, but will not easily be conquered; such a soul will fight it out to the death. Communion with God furnishes the soul with the greatest and choicest arguments to withstand Satan’s temptations. Communion is the result of union. Communion is a mutual exchange between Christ and a gracious soul. Communion is Jacob’s ladder, where you have Christ sweetly coming down into the soul, and the soul, by divine influences, sweetly ascending up to Christ. Communion with Christ is very inflaming, raising and strengthening. While Samson kept up his communion#footnote[The sea ebbs and flows, the moon waxes and wanes; so it is with saints in their communion with God.] with God, no enemy could stand before him, but he goes on conquering and to conquer; but when he had fallen in his communion with God, he quickly falls before the plots of his enemies. It will be so with your souls: so long as your communion with God is kept up, you will be too hard for “the spiritual forces of evil in the heavenly realms”; but if you fall from your#footnote[Plutarch tells of Eudoxus that he would be willing to be burned up at once by the sun, if he might be allowed to come so near it as to learn its nature. What should we not be content to suffer for the keeping up of communion with Christ?] communion with God, you will fall like others before the face of every temptation. David, so long as he kept up his communion with God, stands and triumphs over all his enemies; but when he had fallen in his communion with God, then he falls before the enemies that were in his own bosom, and flees before those who pursued his life. It will be so with your souls if you do not keep up your communion with God. Job keeps up his communion with God, and conquers Satan on the dunghill. Adam loses his communion with God, and is conquered by Satan in Paradise. Communion with God is a shield on land as well as an anchor at sea; it is a sword to defend you as well as a staff to support you. Therefore keep up your communion.

9\. If you would not be taken in any of Satan’s snares, then do not engage Satan in your own strength, but#footnote[That is a remarkable saying of Moses, Exod 15: God is #emph[fortitudo mea,] and #emph[laus mea,] and #emph[salus mea,] my strength, and my praise, and my salvation, all in the abstract.] be every day drawing new power and strength from the Lord Jesus. Certainly the soul that engages any old or new temptation without new strength, new influences from on high, will fall before the power of the temptation. You may see this in Peter. He rested on some old strength he had received (“Though all men should deny you, yet I will not”), and therefore he falls sadly before a new temptation: he curses and#footnote[Matt 26.] swears, and denies him three times who had three times appeared gloriously to him. Ah souls! When the snare is spread, look up to Jesus Christ, who is lifted up in the gospel as the bronze serpent was#footnote[It is only “look up and live”; “Look to Me and be saved, all the ends of the earth,” Isa 45:22.] in the wilderness, and say to him, “Dear Lord, here is a new snare laid to catch my soul, and the grace I received before, without fresh supplies from your blessed bosom, will not deliver me from this snare. Oh, give me new strength, new power, new influences, new measures of grace, so that I may escape this snare.” Ah souls! Remember this: your#super[+]#footnote[John 15:5 χωρὶς ἐμοῦ is #emph[seorsim a me separat,] from me, or apart from me, you#super[+] can do nothing.] strength to stand and overcome must not be expected from graces received, but from the fresh and renewed influences of heaven. You must lean more on Christ than on your duties; you must lean more on Christ than on spiritual tastes and revelations; you must lean more on Christ than on your graces, or else Satan will lead you into captivity, etc.

Tenthly and lastly, if you would not be taken in any of Satan’s snares, then be much in prayer. Prayer is a shelter to the soul, a sacrifice to God and a scourge to the devil. David’s heart was often more out of tune than his harp: he prays, and then, in spite#footnote[Of Carolus Magnus \[Charlemagne\] it was said, #emph[Carolus plus cum Deo quam cum hominibus loquitur,] that he spoke more with God than with men. Ah, that I could say so of the Christians in our days!] of the devil, cries, “Return to your rest, O my soul.” Prayer is (#emph[Porta coeli, clavis Paradisi]) the gate of heaven, a key to let us into Paradise. There is nothing that makes plots fruitless like prayer; therefore Christ says, “Watch and pray so that you#super[+] will not enter into temptation.” You must watch and pray, and pray and watch, if you would not enter into temptation. When Sennacherib and Haman had laid plots and snares to destroy the Jews, they prayed, and their souls were delivered, and Sennacherib and Haman destroyed. David had many snares laid for him, and this drives him to prayer: “Keep me,” he says, “from the snares they have laid for me, #footnote[Ps 141:9, 10.] and from the lures of evildoers. Let the wicked fall into their own nets, while I escape.” “The proud,” he says, “hide a snare for me; the cords of their net are spread along the path, and lures are set #footnote[Ps 140:5, 6.] out for me. Selah. I say to the #smallcaps[Lord], ‘You are my God.’ Hear, O #smallcaps[Lord], my cry for help.”#footnote[#emph[Nunquam abs te, absque te recedo,] Bern. “O Lord,” he says, “I never go away from you without you.”] Saul and many others had laid snares for David, and this drives him to prayer; and so the snares are broken, and he is delivered. Ah souls! Take words with you#super[+], and tell God that Satan has spread his snares in all places and in all companies; tell God that he digs deep, and that he has plot upon plot and device upon device, and all to#footnote[“Let us,” says Basil, “with a holy impudence, make God ashamed, so that he cannot look us in the face, if he denies our persistent pleading; and, like Jacob, say, ‘I will not let you go unless you bless me.’”] ruin you; tell God that you have neither skill nor power to escape his snares; tell God that it is a work too high and too hard for any created being to work your deliverance, unless he puts his own everlasting arms under you; tell God how his honor is engaged to stand by you and to bring you off safe, so that you are not ruined by Satan’s plots; tell God how the wicked would triumph if you should fall into Satan’s snares; tell God of the love of Christ, of the blood of Christ, and of the intercession of Christ for you, so that a way may be found for your escape; tell God that if he will make it his honor to save you from falling into Satan’s snares, you will make it your glory to speak of his goodness and to live out his kindness. Christians must do as Daedalus did, who, when he could not escape by a way on earth, went by a way of heaven; and that is the way of prayer, which is the only way left to escape Satan’s snares, etc.

The next use is a use of thankfulness, for those who escape Satan’s snares, who are not taken by him at his will. Ah Christians! It is your#super[+] duty, with that princely prophet David, to call on your souls and say, “Bless the #footnote[Ps 103:1, 2.] #smallcaps[Lord], O our souls; all that is within us, bless His holy name. Bless the #smallcaps[Lord], O our souls, and do not forget all His kind deeds,” who has not given us up to be a prey to Satan, and to be ensnared by the snares that he has laid for our souls. The sense of this great favor worked up David’s heart to praises: “Blessed be the #smallcaps[Lord],” he says,#footnote[Ps 124:6, 7.] “who has not given us as prey to their teeth. We have escaped like a bird from the snare of the fowler; the snare is broken, and we have escaped.” Ah Christians! Remember that the greatest part of the world, indeed the greatest part of professing Christians, are taken in Satan’s snares. Can you#super[+] think seriously of this and not#footnote[The ancients used to say, #emph[ingratum dixeris omnia dixeris,] call a man unthankful, and you have called him everything.] blush to be unthankful? What are you#super[+] better than others? And what have you#super[+] deserved of God, or done for God, more than others, that you should by the help of a divine hand escape the snares, when others are taken and held in the snares of the devil, to their eternal#footnote[Ps 71:14. “I will praise You more and more”; in the original it is, “I will add to Your praise.”] overthrow, etc.?

Will you#super[+] be thankful for escaping the snares that men spread for your lives or property, etc.? And will you#super[+] not be much more thankful for escaping the snares that Satan has laid for your precious souls?

Remember this: deliverance#footnote[The stork is said to leave one of her young ones where she hatches them, and the elephant to turn up the first sprig toward heaven when he comes to feed, out of some instinct of gratitude. Ah souls, may these not bear witness against you#super[+] in the day of Christ!] from Satan’s snares carries with it the clearest and the greatest evidence that the soul and heart of God are toward you. Many a man, by a common hand of providence, escapes many a snare that man has laid for him, and yet does not escape the snares that Satan has laid for him. Saul and Judas and Demas no doubt escaped many snares that men had laid for them, but none of them escaped the snares that the devil had laid for them. Many men are lifted above the snares of men by a common hand of providence who are left to fall into the snares of the devil by a hand of justice. Your#super[+] deliverance from Satan’s snares is a fruit of special love. Can you#super[+] look on it like this and not be thankful, O precious souls? I think not.

The last use of this point is to call on Christians to long to be at home. Oh, long to be in the bosom of Christ; long to be in the land of Canaan; for#footnote[Augustine wished that he might have seen three things: Rome flourishing, Paul preaching, and Christ conversing with men on the earth. Bede comes after and, correcting this last wish, says, “Yes, but let me see the King in his beauty, Christ in his heavenly kingdom.”] this world, this wilderness, is full of snares; all employments are full of snares, and all enjoyments are full of snares. In the things of this life Satan has his snares to entrap us, and in all spiritual things Satan has his snares to catch us. All places are full of snares: city and country, shop and private chamber, sea and land; and all our mercies are surrounded with snares. There are snares about our tables and snares about our beds, etc. Indeed, Satan is so powerful and subtle that he will often make our greatest, nearest and dearest mercies become our greatest snares. Sometimes he will make the wife who lies in a man’s bosom a snare to him, as Samson’s was, and as Job’s was; sometimes he will make the child a snare, as Absalom was, and Eli’s sons were; and sometimes he will make the servant a snare, as Joseph was to his mistress. Ah souls! Satan is so cunning and artful that he can turn your#super[+] cups into snares, and your clothes into snares, and your houses into snares, and your gardens into snares, and all your recreations into snares, etc. And oh, how the consideration of these things should move all your#super[+] souls to say with the church, “Make haste, my #footnote[Song 8:14 ברח דודי #emph[Berahh dodi,] flee away speedily, my beloved.] beloved, and be like a gazelle or a young stag on the mountains of spices,” and to love and look and long for the coming of Christ! Shall the betrothed maiden long for the wedding day, the servant for his freedom, the captive for his ransom, the traveler for his inn, the#footnote[Phil 1:23. 2 Cor 5:2, 4.] sailor for his harbor? And shall not the people of the Lord long much more to be in the bosom of Christ? There is nothing below the bosom of Christ that is not surrounded with Satan’s snares.

What Paul once said of chains and#footnote[Acts 20:23.] afflictions, that they awaited him in every place, all the saints may say of Satan’s snares, that they await them in every place. This should make them cry out (#emph[migremus hinc, migremus hinc]), “Let us go from here, let us go from here,” and say with Monica, Augustine’s mother, #footnote[Quid hic faciemus? cur non ocyus migramus? cur non hinc avolamus?] “What are we doing here? Why do we not depart from here? Why do we not fly away more swiftly?” Ah souls! Until you#super[+] are taken up into the bosom of Christ, your comforts will not be full, pure and constant. Until then Satan will always be tempting you and spreading snares to entangle you; therefore you should always be crying out with the church, “Come, Lord #footnote[Rev 22:20. It is as easy to encompass the heavens with a hand’s span, and to contain the sea in a nutshell, as to relate fully Christ’s excellencies, or heaven’s happiness.] Jesus!” Is not Christ that star of Jacob that gives light to those who are in darkness? That Prince of Peace, who brings the olive branch of peace to souls that are perplexed? Is not the greatest worth and wealth in him? Are not all the lesser excellencies and perfections of all created beings summed up in him? Is he not the crown of crowns, the glory of glories and the heaven of heavens? Oh then, be always longing after a full, clear and constant enjoyment of Christ in heaven; for until then, Satan will always have plots and designs on you. He acts by an untiring power, and will never let you rest until you are taken up to an everlasting rest in the bosom of Christ.
